{{also|expédient}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{PIE root|en|h₂er|ped}}
From {{inh|en|enm|expedient}}, from {{der|en|fro|expedient}}, from {{der|en|la|expediens}} (stem ''expedient-),'' present participle of {{m|la|expedire|t=to bring forward, to dispatch, to [[expedite]]; impers. to be profitable, serviceable, advantageous, expedient}}, from {{m|la|ex||out}} + {{m|la|pēs|t=foot, hoof}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ɪkˈspiːdi.ənt/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-expedient.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Suitable to effect some desired end or the purpose intended.
#* {{RQ:Authorized Version|John|16|7}}
#*: Nevertheless I tell you the truth; It is '''expedient''' for you that I go away; for if I go not away, the Comforter willnot come unto you; but if I depart, I will send him unto you.
#* {{rfdate|}} {{w|Richard Whately}}
#*: Nothing but the right can ever be '''expedient''', since that can never be true expediency which would sacrifice a greater good to a less.
#: ''Most people, faced with a decision, will choose the most '''expedient''' option.''
# Affording short-term benefit, often at the expense of the long-term.
#* {{quote-journal|lang=en|journal=CQ Weekly|volume=67|issue=31-36|page=2190|
https://books.google.com/books?id=eIJWAAAAYAAJ|year=2009|passage=That's because the debate pits textbook economics — which argues that bailouts beget bad behavior begets more bailouts — against practical politics. And politics, or the taking of '''expedient''' steps to keep people happy, will almost always win.}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|title=The Secret Team: The CIA and Its Allies in Control of the United States and the World|https://books.google.com/books?isbn=1616082844|author=L. Fletcher Prouty, ‎{{w|Jesse Ventura}}|year=2011|passage=Government has slowly but positively moved from an active course of following plans and policies to the easier and more '''expedient''' course of the counterpuncher.}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|title=Immigration Policy In The Federal Republic Of Germany|
https://books.google.com/books?isbn=0857456261|author=Douglas B. Klusmeyer, ‎Demetrios G. Papademetriou|year=2013|passage=Its policies toward foreign lab or across these eras reflect these sharp differences in context, but also reflect a common pattern to treat the recruitment and deployment of foreign nationals as an '''expedient''' measure to serve immediate economic objectives}}
# Governed by [[self-interest]], often short-term self-interest.
#* '''1861''', John Stuart Mill, ''[[s:Utilitarianism|Utilitarianism]]''
#*: But the Expedient, in the sense in which it is opposed to the Right, generally means that which is '''expedient''' for the particular interest of the agent himself; as when a minister sacrifices the interests of his country to keep himself in place.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Quick; rapid; [[expeditious]].
#* ''a'' '''1623''', {{w|Shakespeare}}, {{w|King John}}, Act II, scene 1, lines 57-61
#*: the adverse winds&nbsp;/ Whose leisure I have stay'd, have given him time&nbsp;/ To land his legions all as soon as I;&nbsp;/ His marches are '''expedient''' to this town&nbsp;/ His forces strong, his soldiers confident.

